te":"2019","tourism":"artwork"}],["n",8952176986,39.026559,-77.070823,{"artist_name":"Jason Swain;Thomas Ruttka;Max Stumpf;Beth Meyer","artwork_type":"mural","description":"These concrete piers were remnants from a previous concrete plant. Once the Dog Run Park was complete, the Town wanted to paint them, but they were full of graffiti. The Town decided to paint dog art and enlisted local artists to enhance them.","image":"https://tok.md.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/Dog-Run-2-Art-Walk.jpg","name":"Who Let the Dogs Out","start_date":"2019","tourism":"artwork"}],["n",8960320673,39.059566,-77.094626,{"historic":"ruins","name":"Abandoned Gaynor Road bridge"}],["n",9124947680,39.002738,-77.065052,{"historic":"ruins","name":"Old Ice House"}],["n",10014666972,39.024651,-77.07428,{"artist_name":"Jason Swain","artwork_type":"statue","description":"Carved from white pine.   This hawk was originally inside Town Hall for many years until it found its home on Hawk Island.","image":"https://tok.md.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/02/6AI-xGzxjKFAcZL1JPGRxh6gaSticpQQnknuWtpFF18Ii4dtLKJL0eMuuj_rUhkkfuomdGoYQ0S1WfyBir_TKkgljVlVsGpwxQ1u8xWpyDPf0GWhL9dO5QN9y5ULU4iJ7eZqocT89SmmY_epUIlJPoBnNfbzGSoHatkaubg1gnFZyC2CtpaV7StvzD_Ym_QzeKOBHq2w.png","name":"Hawk Island Hawk","start_date":"2022","tourism":"artwork"}],["n",10049620615,39.024322,-77.071184,{"artist_name":"Jason Swain","artwork_type":"statue","description":"Five birds are featured on this carving to include a woodpecker, blue jay, cardinal, oriole and a hawk on the top. Carved from America Ash.","image":"https://tok.md.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/02/6AI-xGzzCx7_vmxlMea4i8WlxKaGTQKQ5D0DDoy_Fd10fqKS2LVU0GIL11UQWHlXQF4f354kdvgCvgwAwdJuulP777lUipiljgXMFzOgPowGzVzPtAqyM5jdanNLwl1MyfP8OraFIYXu3v4OgAm60bQFWtb89QI9S5HcI88iYR-IL95QcL1KwNAbDKtNiC_lgyYb4Sqs.png","name":"Neighborhood Birds","start_date":"2021","tourism":"artwork"}],["n",10118106497,39.029036,-77.103058,{"artist_name":"Jeff Chyatte","artwork_type":"sculpture","name":"Sonata","tourism":"artwork"}],["n",10118106498,39.032176,-77.103613,{"artist_name":"Roger White Stoller","artwork_type":"sculpture","name":"Tetra Con Brio","tourism":"artwork"}],["n",10659842114,39.028206,-77.077332,{"artwork_type":"photo","description":"10123 Connecticut Avenue: A new Warner Memorial Presbyterian Church takes the place of the earlier one.","image":"https://www.hmdb.org/Photos4/422/Photo422672o.jpeg","name":"Warner Memorial Presbyterian Church","start_date":"1901","tourism":"artwork"}],["n",10659842115,39.028203,-77.077313,{"artwork_type":"photo","description":"Dedication of the National Guard Armory at 3710 Mitchell Street. It housed the National Guard, the Fire Department, and then the Motor Vehicle Administration. It was purchased from the state and has been the Kensington Town Hall since 1976.","image":"https://www.hmdb.org/Photos4/422/Photo422749o.jpg","name":"Dedication of the National Guard Armory","start_date":"1927","tourism":"artwork"}],["n",10659842116,39.0282,-77.077294,{"artwork_type":"photo","description":"The Noyes Library at 10237 Carroll Place was established by Brainard Warner and Crosby Noyes in 1893 to serve Kensington residents. The oldest public library in the Washington, DC, area, it later was converted into a children's library within the Montgome","image":"https://www.hmdb.org/Photos4/422/Photo422277o.jpg","name":"The Noyes Library","start_date":"1977","tourism":"artwork"}],["n",10686981838,39.028196,-77.077275,{"artwork_type":"photo","description":"Howard Avenue has served as the commercial area in Kensington since the town began. The buildings have housed various businesses, and in the 1960s, Howard Avenue began to develop into Antique Row.","image":"https://www.hmdb.org/Photos4/422/Photo422276o.jpg","name":"Howard Avenue","start_date":"1940","tourism":"artwork"}],["n",10686981839,39.028193,-77.077254,{"artwork_type":"photo","description":"The Kensington Volunteer Fire Department. Volunteer fire fighters began organizing in 1894, and s